        "note": "The reference article states Bloop was an ultra-low frequency underwater sound detected by NOAA in 1997 using hydrophones.",
        "correction": null
      },
      {
        "claim": "The signal was picked up by sensors separated by over 3,000 miles.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "The reference notes the sound was heard on multiple sensors at a range of over 5,000 km (3,000 miles), and Wolman's report mentions sensors up to 4,800 km (3,000 miles) apart.",
        "correction": null
      },
      {
        "claim": "The audio profile matched organic living creatures rather than machinery or earthquakes.",
        "segment_index": 2,
